<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; font-size: 2em; margin-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 0 0 20px 0; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Time for Exercise</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Can Swim.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Receptive: Comprehend the gist of short stories and news items. Interactive: Engage in simple discussions on lessons or current events.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Can comprehend the gist of short stories about exercise (e.g., "The story is about a group of friends who play sports."). Can participate in simple discussions about different types of exercises (e.g., "What exercises do you like?" "I like to swim."). Can summarize the gist of news items related to health (e.g., "This news is about being healthy.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; font-size: 1.5em; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 10px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by displaying various pictures of people doing different exercises (e.g., swimming, running, playing football, dancing, hiking). The teacher asks students, "What do you see?" and "What are they doing?" The teacher then mimes actions like swimming, running, and jumping, and students shout out the activity. This activates prior knowledge and introduces key vocabulary visually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a picture of someone swimming and says, "She can swim." The teacher then points to a picture of someone running and says, "He can run." The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary like 'swim', 'run', 'dance', 'football', 'hike' and specifically models /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim," asking students to repeat chorally and individually. The teacher also introduces "wellness" and "exciting", segmenting them as "well-ness" and "ex-cit-ing".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses Concept Checking Questions to ensure understanding.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of swimming) "Is this reading a book?" (No) "Is this swimming?" (Yes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of running) "Can she fly?" (No) "Can she run?" (Yes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'healthy' good for you or bad for you?" (Good for you).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio story (30-45 seconds) about friends doing different exercises in the afternoon, incorporating new vocabulary and grammar. For example: "It's Saturday afternoon. My friend Leo and I like to be active. I like swimming, and Leo likes playing football. Sometimes, we go running in the park. My friend Maria likes to dance, and she says it's very fun and makes her feel healthy. We all can do many exciting sports to stay fit every day!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend the gist of short stories.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening 1:</b> The teacher plays the audio story once. Students listen and then point to the picture that best shows what the story is about (e.g., a group of friends doing sports vs. a single person reading). Teacher asks, "What is the story about? Friends playing sports, or one person reading?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening 2:</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students raise their hands when they hear a word related to exercise.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher plays the audio once more. Students listen and then indicate if simple statements are true or false (e.g., "The story is about animals." - False; "The story is about friends who exercise." - True).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific details, using modal verbs (can, cannot), present simple questions and affirmative sentences ("Do you like sports? Yes, I do."), WH-questions + answer + subject + verb + ing ("What type of exercise do you like? I like swimming."), and core vocabulary (walk, exercise, afternoon, gym, sport, activity, friend, football, swimming, running, hiking, dancing, cycling, go, swim, play, run, dance, healthy, active, fun, exciting, every day, sometimes).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Listen and Circle):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with a list of words from the audio story mixed with distractors (e.g., "swim, read, football, sleep, dance, sing, run, eat, hike, clean"). Students listen to the audio story again (twice). Every time they hear an exercise word from the story, they circle it on their worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Listen and Match):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with three short sentences related to the audio story on one side and three pictures (e.g., someone swimming, someone playing football, someone dancing) on the other. Students listen to the audio story again and draw a line to match each sentence to the correct picture. Example sentences: "Leo likes playing football." "Maria likes to dance." "I like swimming."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. They listen to the audio for a final time. After listening, they ask each other simple questions based on the story and their understanding. "What type of exercise does Leo like?" (He likes playing football.) "Does Maria like dancing?" (Yes, she does. She likes dancing.) "Is swimming fun?" (Yes, it is. I like swimming.) This allows them to compare their answers from previous activities and reinforce their listening comprehension and simple oral responses. The teacher encourages sociolinguistic phrases like "You can do it!" if a peer is struggling.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and Draw an Exercise Schedule." In pairs, students listen to a slightly modified or new short audio segment (e.g., "On Monday afternoon, I like to swim. On Tuesday, I play football with my friend. On Wednesday, we go running in the park. On Thursday, I ride my bike. On Friday, I dance.") They are given a simple weekly schedule template (Monday-Friday boxes) and must listen carefully to draw a small picture representing the exercise for each day.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains the task: "Listen to the audio. What exercise does the person do each day? Draw it in the box for that day."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. The teacher plays the audio twice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening, pairs compare their drawings and orally state what they heard: "On Monday, I can see swimming." "Yes, he likes swimming on Monday." (Practicing "can" and "I like + verb-ing").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages students to ask about preferences: "Do you like to swim?" "I like swimming." (Pragmatic function).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Listen and Draw" activity and during the pair comparison, focusing on listening accuracy and simple oral responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays a new, very short audio clip (15-20 seconds) describing a simple activity. Students listen and then choose the correct picture (from a set of three) that matches the activity described. For example, audio: "My friends and I always go running in the park every day. It makes us feel healthy." Pictures: 1) kids reading, 2) kids running, 3) kids eating. Students circle picture 2.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in choosing the correct picture based on listening comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify target vocabulary (e.g., 'running', 'healthy') from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim" during pair sharing (if applicable).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Simple expression of preferences when prompted by teacher or peer (e.g., "I like swimming.").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer two questions on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new exercise word you heard today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you understand stories about exercise?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy for you today? What was difficult?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can listening to English stories help you learn more?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to identify the gist of the story and some specific details. The visual support was very helpful for comprehension. Some students struggled with differentiating between 'running' and 'swimming' when simply listening, requiring more pronunciation practice and visual association. I need to ensure clearer segmenting of multisyllabic words like "exciting" next time and reinforce "can" and "cannot" more explicitly. For the next lesson, I will provide more scaffolding for listening by introducing a visual aid related to the reading text beforehand.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This listening lesson introduced key vocabulary about exercise. In Lesson 2, students will read a short text using this same vocabulary to further build their comprehension skills, focusing on reading comprehension, and see the words in written context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>